/* rnl-std-no-labels 0.4, a package of the standard series 0.4 (16.3).
   Made from the files of the series 0.3 (libraries/standard/0.3), which say what they were
   generated from, and edited in rnl, whose files are the source.
   Turns the default labels of the standard symbols off, for a file that writes its own (16.3).
   Optional, in no collection: a file names it with USE-PACKAGE. */
@RNL(type="header") { @VERSION("0.4"); @PACKAGE(name="rnl-std-no-labels", version="0.4")}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- rnl-std-no-labels: no default labels
   Not implicit. A file whose labels are all its own, such as one an editor writes, names it with
   USE-PACKAGE in its header. Every label the standard symbols give an instance or a usage (name,
   value, model) becomes empty; the file's own TEXT with the same key comes later and shows it again
   (13.3). A usage drawn with a device's symbol, such as a $FIGURE with $RESISTOR, has no value or
   model to show, and its label would fail (13.6); empty, it reads nothing. A block's formula stays:
   it is part of the drawing. A symbol without one of these keys gets an empty label for it, which
   draws nothing. Only objects that are drawn are selected (drawn=true, 10.1, 16.3), so that no empty
   text lands on a usage without a symbol, such as a differential pair. */
@RNL {
  # @SELECT-INSTANCE(drawn=true, recursive=true) => @TEXT("", key="name");
  # @SELECT-INSTANCE(drawn=true, recursive=true) => @TEXT("", key="value");
  # @SELECT-INSTANCE(drawn=true, recursive=true) => @TEXT("", key="model");
  # @SELECT-USAGE(drawn=true, recursive=true) => @TEXT("", key="name");
  # @SELECT-USAGE(drawn=true, recursive=true) => @TEXT("", key="value");
  # @SELECT-USAGE(drawn=true, recursive=true) => @TEXT("", key="model")
}
